 TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  08 REMOVED_MONTH 98 at 10 13 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  additional inferolateral margin  is a 2 x 2 x 1cm fragment of fibrofatty tissue inked black and over inked green at the new inferolateral margin  Representative section frozen as FX  1  now in A  Remainder of tissue submitted in B and C  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left breast mass  is a 5 x 4 x 1 5cm oriented fragment of breast tissue inked as follows  anterior and posterior  black  superior and lateral  green  and inferolateral  red  inferomedial  blue  superomedial  yellow  The specimen is sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm healing biopsy cavity with central hemorrhagic area and surrounding firm  tan white tissue present 0 2cm from the anterior margin and 0 3cm from the posterior margin  Sections submitted as follows  D  Biopsy cavity with anterior and posterior margins  E  Biopsy cavity with posterior margin  F  Firm tissue and inferolateral posterior margins  G  Biopsy cavity with anterior  skin  and posterior margins  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  left axillary contents  is a REMOVED_DATE x 3 x 2cm fragment of adipose tissue from which lymph nodes are dissected and submitted in cassettes H through L  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  additional axillary tissue  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm fragment of tan white  rubbery tissue  submitted entirely in M  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  level 3 node  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm aggregate of adipose tissue and an associated REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 3cm white  rubbery  query lymph node  submitted entirely in cassette N after bisection  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  additional lateral tissue  is a focally cauterized fragment of pink  rubbery tissue measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm  The specimen is inked black serially sectioned  submitted entirely in cassettes O through Q  Resident  JUSTIN M  CATES  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1   ADDITIONAL INFEROLATERAL MARGIN   INVASIVE CARCINOMA  EXTENDING TO NEW INFEROLATERAL MARGIN  O R  Resident  RHONDA YANTISS  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Robert Henry Young  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Barbara Lynn Smith  M D  PART A  BREAST  LEFT   RE EXCISION PART B  LYMPH NODES  LEFT AXI